# How to study a binary mixture ?

Considering mixtures, there are 3 possible outcomes in terms of toxic effects :

-   **Additivity** : Effect(A) + Effect(B) = Effect(A+B) (no interaction)
-   **Antagonisme** : Effect(A) + Effect(B) \> Effect(A+B) (positive interaction)
-   **Synergy** : Effect(A) + Effect(B) \< Effect(A+B) (negative interaction)

In ecotoxicology, the last case in the most concerning one as security levels determined on single substances can be insufficient to protect the environment.

## Graphical representations

As seen in the last part, when only one substance is studied at a time it gives us dose-reponse **curves** (2D : Dose x Effect). But when we study a binary mixture, we are looking to define a dose-response **surface** (3D : Dose substance A x Dose substance B x Effect). This surface can be displayed in 3D but also in 2D, under the same principle of level line in maps (@fig-schemedrcs).

